# Optimization Strategies Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines establish patterns for optimizing application performance across all layers. While specific optimization techniques may vary by feature, these core principles should guide all optimization efforts.

## Best Practices
- Always measure before optimizing - use Flutter DevTools to identify bottlenecks
- Focus on user-perceived performance first (UI responsiveness)
- Optimize critical paths that impact user experience
- Balance code readability with performance - document complex optimizations
- Avoid premature optimization
- Implement performance monitoring in production
- Set performance budgets for key metrics (startup time, memory usage, frame rate)
- Profile on low-end devices
- Document performance requirements and test results
- Implement performance regression tests for critical features

## Layer-Specific Optimization Strategies

### UI/Widget Optimization
- Minimize widget rebuilds using `const` constructors
- Use `RepaintBoundary` to isolate frequently changing widgets
- Implement widget caching for expensive build operations
- Utilize `ListView.builder` instead of Column for long lists
- Employ lazy loading for off-screen content
- Use appropriate image formats and resolutions
- Minimize shader compilation jank with shader warming
- Avoid expensive operations in build() methods
- Optimize state management to prevent unnecessary rebuilds
- Use `computeAsync` for expensive UI calculations
- Implement `shouldRepaint` in custom painters to avoid unnecessary repaints
- Override `operator ==` and `hashCode` for custom widget equality comparison
- Reduce build method complexity by extracting methods
- Use `SizedBox` instead of `Container` when only dimensions are needed
- Consider using `AnimationBuilder` for custom animations to localize rebuilds
- Implement `FittedBox` for responsive layout adaptations
- Example of optimized list:
  ```dart
  // GOOD: Optimized list with builder and fixed item extent
  ListView.builder(
    itemCount: items.length,
    itemExtent: 60.0, // Fixed item height improves performance
    itemBuilder: (context, index) {
      return ListTile(
        key: ValueKey(items[index].id),
        title: Text(items[index].title),
      );
    },
  );
  ```

### State Management Optimization
- Use selective rebuilds with state management solutions:
  - `Provider.select()` to listen to specific state changes
  - `BlocBuilder` with conditional rebuild parameters
  - `Selector` widgets to optimize rebuilds
- Implement `ValueNotifier` for simple state that requires minimal rebuilds
- Use `InheritedWidget` efficiently for passing data down widget tree
- Consider state immutability to improve comparison performance
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // GOOD: Selective rebuilds with Provider
  Consumer<UserState>(
    builder: (context, userState, child) {
      return Text(userState.username);
    },
    // Only rebuild when username changes
    selector: (context, userState) => userState.username,
  );
  
  // GOOD: Conditional rebuilds with BlocBuilder
  BlocBuilder<CounterBloc, CounterState>(
    buildWhen: (previous, current) => previous.count != current.count,
    builder: (context, state) {
      return Text('${state.count}');
    },
  );
  ```

### Data Layer Optimization
- Implement efficient caching strategies
- Use pagination for large data sets
- Optimize database queries and index critical fields
- Batch network requests when possible
- Implement efficient serialization/deserialization
- Use appropriate data structures for different access patterns
- Compress data when appropriate
- Implement delta updates for large data sets
- Optimize JSON parsing with code generation tools
- Minimize deep object copying
- Use `isolate_handler` or `compute` for heavy operations
- Implement data prefetching for anticipated user actions
- Example of optimized data access:
  ```dart
  // GOOD: Using compute for CPU-intensive operations
  Future<List<FilteredItem>> filterItems(List<Item> items, Filter filter) async {
    return compute(_filterItems, {
      'items': items,
      'filter': filter,
    });
  }
  
  // Pure function for compute
  List<FilteredItem> _filterItems(Map<String, dynamic> params) {
    final items = params['items'] as List<Item>;
    final filter = params['filter'] as Filter;
    
    // CPU-intensive filtering
    return items.where((item) => filter.matches(item)).toList();
  }
  ```

### Asset Optimization
- Compress images appropriately
- Use vector graphics (SVG) when possible
- Implement asset preloading for critical resources
- Implement proper image caching
- Optimize font loading and usage
- Use appropriate asset resolutions for different devices
- Consider using sprite sheets for multiple small images
- Lazy load non-critical assets
- Use `CachedNetworkImage` for efficient network image caching
- Implement placeholders for images during loading
- Consider custom image loading strategies for critical paths
- Example:
  ```dart
  // GOOD: Optimized image loading with placeholder and error handling
  CachedNetworkImage(
    imageUrl: imageUrl,
    placeholder: (context, url) => Container(
      color: Colors.grey[300],
      child: Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),
    ),
    errorWidget: (context, url, error) => Icon(Icons.error),
    fadeInDuration: Duration(milliseconds: 300),
    fit: BoxFit.cover,
  )
  ```

### Memory Management
- Properly dispose controllers, animations, and streams
- Implement lazy initialization for expensive resources
- Use weak references for caching objects that might be garbage collected
- Release resources when navigating away from screens
- Monitor memory usage during development
- Avoid memory leaks from closures and callbacks
- Example of proper resource management:
  ```dart
  class MyWidget extends StatefulWidget {
    @override
    _MyWidgetState createState() => _MyWidgetState();
  }
  
  class _MyWidgetState extends State<MyWidget> with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin {
    late AnimationController _controller;
    StreamSubscription? _subscription;
    
    @override
    void initState() {
      super.initState();
      _controller = AnimationController(vsync: this);
      _subscription = stream.listen((_) {});
    }
    
    @override
    void dispose() {
      // GOOD: Proper resource cleanup
      _controller.dispose();
      _subscription?.cancel();
      super.dispose();
    }
    
    @override
    Widget build(BuildContext context) {
      return Container();
    }
  }
  ```

## Performance Monitoring
- Implement custom performance traces for critical operations
- Track key performance metrics:
  - Application startup time
  - Time to first meaningful paint
  - Frame rendering time
  - Memory usage
  - Network request latency
  - Battery consumption
- Set up alerting for performance regressions
- Regularly review performance dashboards
- Document performance improvements and regressions
- Use Flutter Performance overlay during development
- Implement custom instrumentation for critical user journeys
- Add trace markers for performance debugging:
  ```dart
  import 'dart:developer' as developer;
  
  Future<void> performExpensiveOperation() async {
    developer.Timeline.startSync('ExpensiveOperation');
    try {
      // Expensive operation code
    } finally {
      developer.Timeline.finishSync();
    }
  }
  ```

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Implement automated performance tests for critical features
- Benchmark against previous versions to prevent regressions
- Test on a range of devices, especially low-end ones
- Validate optimizations with real-world usage patterns
- Test under various network conditions
- Verify memory usage patterns over extended usage
- Use Flutter DevTools to identify and resolve performance issues
- Document baseline performance metrics for key operations
- Run performance tests as part of CI/CD pipeline when feasible
- Create specific tests for rendering performance:
  ```dart
  testWidgets('List scrolling maintains 60fps', (WidgetTester tester) async {
    await tester.pumpWidget(MyApp());
    await tester.pumpAndSettle();
    
    final listFinder = find.byType(ListView);
    final startTime = DateTime.now();
    
    // Scroll multiple times
    for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
      await tester.drag(listFinder, Offset(0, -300));
      await tester.pump(Duration(milliseconds: 16)); // ~60fps
    }
    
    final endTime = DateTime.now();
    final duration = endTime.difference(startTime);
    
    // Verify scrolling was smooth (approximately 60fps)
    expect(duration.inMilliseconds, lessThan(20 * 20)); // 20 frames at max 20ms each
  });
  ```
